@ProcListCfg
$^#Function list -- setup

There are 3 properties:

 #file type# - presentable name of file mask;
 #file mask# - regular expression that define filemask;
 ~Regular expressions~@RegExp@ - expression that is used for function definition.
 ~presentation expression~@RegExp@ - expression that define appearance of found function
 #select from cursor# - if checked, in created menu will be selected nearest to cursor item,
otherwise will be selected previously selected item;
 #sort items# - enables items sorting;
 #shift unused parameters in presentation string# - shifts unused parameters in
Presentation expression.

 For example, this properties set define module's behavior for C/C++ files:

@-
 #File type#
 C/C++ files
 #File mask#
 \.\c{C|H|{CPP}|{HPP}|{CC}|{RH}|{INL}|{XS}}$
 #Regular expression#
 ^{(\w[\w_:*&~~]+[\w\s_:*&~~]@@\([\S\s]*)}|
 {(struct\s*\w*[\S\s]*[^^;])}|{(\##define\s*\S*\([\S\s]*)}$
 #presentation expression#
 \1\2\3
@+

In #presentation expression# you can use special macro's:
 %LINE%      - line number;
 %INDEX%     - function number;
 %PERCENT%   - offset in percents from begin of file.

 Notes.
 I can implement "file mask" processing as in FAR (simple filemasks
delimited with commas), but service function CmpName doesn't process similar masks.
For me has appeared easier to make processing using RegExp's :)


~Copyrights~@Copyrights@

@ProcListCfgMenu
$^#Menu with filetypes

 Ins - add new filetype;
 Del - delete filetype;
 F4 - edit currently selected filetype.


